# Adopting what already exists.
#
# Four Vercel projects and one Supabase project were created by hand, before any
# of this was written down. Terraform does not know about them, so its first
# `plan` wants to CREATE all five — which fails on the name collisions, and, if
# it somehow did not, would give you a second set of everything.
#
# Copy this file to imports.tf, fill in the real ids, run ONE plan, and delete
# it again once the ids are in state. Import blocks are declarative (Terraform
# ≥ 1.5): they change nothing on their own, they only tell Terraform "this
# resource is that thing over there".
#
#   cp imports.tf.example imports.tf     # then edit the ids below
#   terraform plan                       # must show 0 to destroy. If it does
#                                        # not, stop and read why.
#   terraform apply
#   rm imports.tf
#
# Where the ids come from:
#   Vercel   — Project → Settings → General → Project ID (prj_…), or `vercel project ls`
#   Supabase — the project ref in the dashboard URL and in the API URL
#              (https://<ref>.supabase.co)
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Read this before you run apply
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# The first apply is not one change, it is five at once: adopting five
# resources, changing the Root Directory of all four Vercel projects, adding a
# preview environment's worth of variables, attaching domains, and (if you let
# it) switching off Vercel's git deployments. On a live production site.
#
# Do it in stages. Each stage is a plan you can read in full:
#
#   1. Imports only. Comment out everything in vercel.tf and supabase.tf that
#      would change an existing attribute, until `plan` says "no changes".
#      That is the moment Terraform's picture matches reality.
#   2. Root Directory. Uncomment it, apply, deploy once by hand, confirm the
#      site and its /api/** routes still answer.
#   3. The preview environment: the second Supabase project, the preview
#      variables, the preview domains. Nothing here touches production.
#   4. Only once a GitHub Actions deploy has gone green end to end:
#      vercel_git_auto_deploy = false, deploy_pipeline_enabled = true.
#
# Between any two stages you can stop, and what is running keeps running.

# import {
#   to = vercel_project.this["admin"]
#   id = "prj_xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx"
# }
#
# import {
#   to = vercel_project.this["staff"]
#   id = "prj_xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx"
# }
#
# import {
#   to = vercel_project.this["widget"]
#   id = "prj_xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx"
# }
#
# The logafi project has never been created — leave it out, and let the first
# apply create it.
#
# import {
#   to = supabase_project.this["production"]
#   id = "xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx"
# }
#
# Domains already attached to a project, if any. The id is "<project-id>/<domain>".
#
# import {
#   to = vercel_project_domain.this["admin-production-dorafi.logafi.com"]
#   id = "prj_xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx/dorafi.logafi.com"
# }
